<section class="three-tool-knowledge" id="travel-deep-guide"><h2>Travel Prayer Guide: Qasr, Jam, journey distances and school differences</h2><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Qasr permits shortening four-rak'ah Fard prayers to two in a qualifying journey. Fajr remains two, Maghrib three. The journey's distance, residence intention and rules for joining prayers differ across schools; a number entered in an app does not establish eligibility.</p><nav class="three-tool-nav" aria-label="Guide sections"><a href="#travel-deep-meaning">What is Qasr and how is it</a><a href="#travel-deep-fard">Which Fard prayers may be </a><a href="#travel-deep-schools">Hanafi, Shafi'i, Maliki an</a><a href="#travel-deep-distance">How far must you travel to</a><a href="#travel-deep-example">Worked Qasr example</a><a href="#travel-deep-sources">Sources</a></nav><div class="three-tool-facts"><div><strong>Ordinary Fard: 17</strong></div><div><strong>Potential Qasr Fard: 11</strong></div><div><strong>Qualification: school-specific</strong></div></div><section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-meaning"><h3>What is Qasr and how is it different from Jam?</h3><p>Qasr is the shortening of the four-unit Dhuhr, Asr and Isha prayers to two in qualifying travel. Jam refers to combining the time of two prayers, and is a different legal question. Quran 4:101 mentions shortening prayer when travelling; schools discuss associated conditions and narrations.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-fard"><h3>Which Fard prayers may be shortened when travelling?</h3><p>The normal Fard counts are Fajr 2, Dhuhr 4, Asr 4, Maghrib 3 and Isha 4. In qualifying Qasr, Dhuhr, Asr and Isha may become 2 each. Fajr stays 2, Maghrib remains 3. The total is 11, but it is not automatic for every trip or in every congregational situation.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-schools"><h3>Hanafi, Shafi'i, Maliki and Hanbali travel rules</h3><p>Hanafi discussions commonly mention a 15-day intended residence distinction, whereas Shafi'i discussions commonly examine around four complete days and arrival/departure exclusions. Maliki and Hanbali rules also consider journey and residence. Do not convert these abbreviated notes into an unconditional rule: local boundaries, intention, fellow worshippers and actual legal opinion matter.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-distance"><h3>How far must you travel to pray Qasr?</h3><p>Distance thresholds are not identical across schools and methods of measuring a journey. A map showing 80 kilometres does not prove you have left your town or that all other conditions are met. Departure from one's settled residence and exceptions involving work, commuting and stopovers need specific qualified advice.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-example"><h3>Worked Qasr example: the five daily prayers</h3><p>Suppose a traveller is confirmed eligible for Qasr under their followed school and prays alone. The ordinary five Fard totals 2 + 4 + 4 + 3 + 4 = 17. The shortened sequence is 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 2 = 11. This example demonstrates the prayer units only; it makes no claim that a particular journey qualifies.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-congregation"><h3>What happens when a traveller joins a resident imam?</h3><p>Following a resident imam and leading a congregation can affect whether a travelling worshipper completes four units rather than shortening. The legal rules differ by school. A user should not apply the standalone table without reviewing the imam's status, their own intention and school guidance.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-timing"><h3>Prayer combining, delayed prayers and time windows</h3><p>Jam' taqdim and Jam' ta'khir are combining arrangements whose eligibility and methods differ across schools. In Hanafi teaching, conventional joining is particularly restricted except in recognised Hajj circumstances; other schools allow other valid journeys. Qasr does not itself specify an iqamah time or permit omitting prayers.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-checklist"><h3>Before you shorten: practical decision checklist</h3><p>Identify your legal school and a qualified reference. Confirm where the journey legally begins, destination and one-way distance, purpose and intended stay, whether you will be praying with a resident imam, and whether you are asking about Qasr or Jam. Then use the comparative table as a reminder rather than a religious verdict.</p></section><section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-faq"><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>{faqs.map(([q,a])=><details class="faq-detail"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}</section><section class="three-tool-section" id="travel-deep-sources"><h3>Sources and further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://quran.com/4/101" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 4:101</a></li><li><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1081" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1081</a></li><li><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1080" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1080</a></li></ul><p class="small">Educational reference, not a binding religious ruling. Qualified scholarly review pending.</p></section></section>